    {
      "id": "HTdV3OLRNGNSOaHRhiv6W",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1852.9692155451967,
      "y": 22386.50840383637,
      "width": 1056,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOb",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 388086277,
      "version": 1130,
      "versionNonce": 770579211,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788791985376,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Do we even need a cache ?\n2. Do we need distributed cache?\n\nA good scaled instance of Redis lets say use something like Azure or AWS, A good scaled\ninstance of Redis can comfortabally handle few GBs of data\n\nElastic Amazon cache can help us to prepare Redis instance or memcache instance prepared\nwe can see here like\ncache.r6g.16xlarge machine having more than 400 GB of space and price of this is approx\n5$ per hour.\n\nPoint here is that, if the data that we want to store is probably like few GBs like 10 GB\nor 20 GB data then we can store that in a single instance pretty easily, we dont need to \nunnecessarily even have a sharded and distributed instance on Redis.\n\nLets say we want to design a Leaderboard where we have 1 Million concurrent users, if lets say\nwe have 1 million users for which we want to store the data. For 1M user, lets say the metadata \nthat we want to store is around 1 KB thats going to be only 1 GB of RAM.\n\n16:00",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Do we even need a cache ?\n2. Do we need distributed cache?\n\nA good scaled instance of Redis lets say use something like Azure or AWS, A good scaled\ninstance of Redis can comfortabally handle few GBs of data\n\nElastic Amazon cache can help us to prepare Redis instance or memcache instance prepared\nwe can see here like\ncache.r6g.16xlarge machine having more than 400 GB of space and price of this is approx\n5$ per hour.\n\nPoint here is that, if the data that we want to store is probably like few GBs like 10 GB\nor 20 GB data then we can store that in a single instance pretty easily, we dont need to \nunnecessarily even have a sharded and distributed instance on Redis.\n\nLets say we want to design a Leaderboard where we have 1 Million concurrent users, if lets say\nwe have 1 million users for which we want to store the data. For 1M user, lets say the metadata \nthat we want to store is around 1 KB thats going to be only 1 GB of RAM.\n\n16:00",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    }
